  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Below normal temperatures with frost and freeze\nconcerns continue through Monday morning.\n\nThe pressure gradient will start increasing from the southeast \nthe second half of the night, but most areas west of I-95 should\nlargely remain decoupled. Therefore, Freeze Warnings and Frost \nAdvisories remain in effect for much of this area (the growing \nseason ended Sunday morning west of the Blue Ridge). The Freeze \nWarning may be somewhat marginal, but thought the western areas \nthat have the lightest winds, lowest dew points, and deeper \nvalleys and basins (i.e., the Culpeper Basin) have a reasonable \nchance of reaching freezing. It's possible some patchy frost may\nform over the rest of the piedmont, though the light wind\ndeveloping after midnight in most guidance keeps confidence too\nlow for a headline expansion as of early evening. Some areas \nmay be colder tonight compared to Sunday morning since \nradiational cooling conditions may be slightly more favorable \n(less wind). Locations west of the Blue Ridge could drop into \nthe 20s.\n\nTemperatures and dew points will climb slightly Monday and \nMonday night, with additional Frost Advisories appearing \nunlikely.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Uncertainty remains in regards to the track and\npotential impacts from a nearby Nor'easter this week.\n\nOverall not too much change in the weather pattern for the upcoming \nworkweek. An \"Omega Block\" remains with troughiness in the eastern \nand western U.S. along with ridging in the center part of the \ncountry. At the surface is a developing Nor'easter off the Carolina\ncoast that will gradually meander north and east throughout the\nweek. With the Omega Block, expect zonal flow aloft and not \nmuch of a west-to-east movement of storm systems across the \ncountry. In turn, this will hold the Nor'easter offshore of the \nMid-Atlantic through mid to late week. \n\nCurrent model guidance still shows subtle differences in \nregards to the placement and timing of the Nor'easter this week,\nalthough some consistency has been made amongst recent model \nruns. The EC is fairly clustered and well offshore, while the \nGEFS/CMCE are slightly closer to the coast. Each of these \nsolutions and their corresponding members seem to keep the low \npressure system far enough offshore with perhaps an isolated \nshower making it as far west as southern MD/western shore of the\nChesapeake Bay and the northern neck of VA mid to late week. \nThe probability for precipitation has trended downward given \nstrong 1034-1038 mb high pressure retreating Monday followed up \nby a secondary 1028-1032 mb wedging Canadian high pressure \nTuesday through Thursday.\n\nAs a result, expect most locations to remain dry with some passing \nclouds from time to time in inland areas as spokes of shortwave \nenergy pivot around the Nor'easter offshore. Each of these \nshortwaves will deliver an uptick in wind out of the north and \nnortheast, mainly during the afternoon and evening periods. Any \nimpactful weather will be tied to coastal communities along the\nChesapeake Bay and points east. Confidence remains medium for \nat least some periods of gusty north/northeast winds and \npotentially some coastal flooding concerns this week. We will \ncontinue to monitor the progress of this developing system. See\nmarine and coastal flooding discussions below. \n\nOver time, the ridging north of the closed low will allow the\nairmass to modify. Highs temperatures will top out in the mid \nto upper 60s (50s mountains) each day with overnight lows in the\nupper 30s to mid 40s. Overnight lows warm into the 50s late week.\n\nThe Nor'easter will pull away Thursday into Friday with an even \nstronger wedging high pressure system building this weekend. Dry \nconditions look to continue for most through middle of next week \nwith changes in the weather pattern once again by the October \n29th-31st timeframe.",
